---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Header from "../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";

const title = "Concrete Driveways and Approaches in Michigan | Faber Concrete";
const description =
  "Five-inch concrete driveways and six-inch approaches on compacted 21AA. Built for Michigan cold and heavy delivery trucks. Request an estimate for April or May.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <Header />
  <main class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="eyebrow">Driveways</p>
      <h1>Driveways and approaches</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The driveway is five inches. The approach — the section that takes the hit from the street — is six. Both sit on 21AA that has been graded and compacted before anyone sets a form.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most concrete contractors pour four inches. Skipping that extra inch is how a bid gets cheaper. Matt Faber puts it plainly: those crews are tripping over dollars to pick up pennies. On an average driveway, one less inch saves them on the order of $600 to $700. Faber Concrete charges for the inch and pours it.
      </p>
      <h2>Why the approach is thicker</h2>
      <p>
        Michigan is a cold climate. An extra inch on the whole driveway, and another inch on the approach, is what you want when the slab has to live through freeze and thaw. Then there is the truck. A 20-ton delivery truck coming in and out of the driveway is a different load than a passenger car. The approach is where that weight lands first, which is why it is six inches.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        A thinner slab can crack enough that you hate the driveway long before you are ready to replace it. The extra inch is there so that does not start early.
      </div>
      <h2>What is under it</h2>
      <p>
        We never pour over dirt. We also do not compact a layer of sand and call it a base. A lot of bids save a few hundred dollars by delivering sand, compacting it, and pouring on top. Sand and the soil under a slab can erode. 21AA, graded across the whole area and compacted, is a more solid base, with less chance of that washing out from underneath.
      </p>
      <p>
        Order of work, every time: grade the 21AA, compact it, then form, then pour five inches — six on the approach.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="btn btn--tide" href="/estimate">Request a driveway estimate</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/why-our-concrete-lasts">The full 5-inch story</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
